                "2": {
                    "name": "text_block",
                    "id": 311,
                    "position_x": 1027,
                    "position_y": 45,
                    "width": 768,
                    "height": 1016,
                    "visibility": "show_all",
                    "draggable": true,
                    "presentation_state": "show_all",
                    "properties": {
                        "0": {
                            "name": "block",
                            "value": "Makes copies of the whole tensor and lays them end to end, along each dimension - tiling.\n\nUse it to fill a larger grid with a pattern, to make a batch of identical copies, or to stretch a single row into a matrix of identical rows (t.repeat 4 1 on a 1 x 10 row gives 4 x 10).\n\nEach count says how many copies to make along one dimension, so t.repeat 2 3 on a 7 x 6 tensor stacks 2 copies down and\n3 across: 14 x 18. This is NOT numpy's repeat, which copies each element next to itself (0 0 1 1 2 2); here the WHOLE tensor is laid end to end (0 1 2 0 1 2).\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.repeat <count: int> <count: int> ...\nUsually one count per dimension (see the counts field below). With no\narguments the field is empty and the tensor is passed through\nunchanged.\n\nEXAMPLE:\nt.repeat 2 3\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nThe tensor (a list or numpy array is converted to a float32 tensor). Sends the copies on arrival. Only this inlet sends.\n\nrepeats (text field, also an inlet):\nThe counts, separated by commas or spaces. There must be AT LEAST as many counts as the tensor has dimensions - fewer prints an error in the console and nothing is sent (t.tile accepts fewer). More counts than dimensions add new dimensions at the front: 2, 1, 1 on a 7 x 6 tensor gives 2 x 7 x 6, two copies stacked along a new first dimension. A count of 1 leaves that dimension alone; 0 empties it. The next tensor uses a change - in the demo a metro inside make_F re-sends the F ten times a second.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nrepeated tensor out:\nThe copies, as one new tensor.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe F (7 x 6, left) tiled 2, 3 gives 14 x 18 on the right - 2 rows of 3 F's. Type other counts: 1, 4 is a row of four, 3, 1 a column of three. t.info shows the shape of the result.",
                            "value_type": "str"
                        },
                        "1": {
                            "name": "lock",
                            "value": true,
                            "value_type": "bool"
                        },
                        "2": {
                            "name": "width",
                            "value": 760,
                            "value_type": "int"
                        },
                        "3": {
                            "name": "height",
                            "value": 1000,
                            "value_type": "int"
                        },
                        "4": {
                            "name": "text_size",
                            "value": "30",
                            "value_type": "str"
                        }
                    },
                    "text": "Makes copies of the whole tensor and lays them end to end, along each dimension - tiling.\n\nUse it to fill a larger grid with a pattern, to make a batch of identical copies, or to stretch a single row into a matrix of identical rows (t.repeat 4 1 on a 1 x 10 row gives 4 x 10).\n\nEach count says how many copies to make along one dimension, so t.repeat 2 3 on a 7 x 6 tensor stacks 2 copies down and\n3 across: 14 x 18. This is NOT numpy's repeat, which copies each element next to itself (0 0 1 1 2 2); here the WHOLE tensor is laid end to end (0 1 2 0 1 2).\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.repeat <count: int> <count: int> ...\nUsually one count per dimension (see the counts field below). With no\narguments the field is empty and the tensor is passed through\nunchanged.\n\nEXAMPLE:\nt.repeat 2 3\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nThe tensor (a list or numpy array is converted to a float32 tensor). Sends the copies on arrival. Only this inlet sends.\n\nrepeats (text field, also an inlet):\nThe counts, separated by commas or spaces. There must be AT LEAST as many counts as the tensor has dimensions - fewer prints an error in the console and nothing is sent (t.tile accepts fewer). More counts than dimensions add new dimensions at the front: 2, 1, 1 on a 7 x 6 tensor gives 2 x 7 x 6, two copies stacked along a new first dimension. A count of 1 leaves that dimension alone; 0 empties it. The next tensor uses a change - in the demo a metro inside make_F re-sends the F ten times a second.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nrepeated tensor out:\nThe copies, as one new tensor.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe F (7 x 6, left) tiled 2, 3 gives 14 x 18 on the right - 2 rows of 3 F's. Type other counts: 1, 4 is a row of four, 3, 1 a column of three. t.info shows the shape of the result."
                },
